      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.942,
            0.05,
            0.987,
            0.069
          ],
          "content": "147"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.101,
            0.447,
            0.138
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 10.6: Let us examine (demonstration activity)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.157,
            0.534,
            0.176
          ],
          "content": "The teacher may demonstrate this activity."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.178,
            0.555,
            0.235
          ],
          "content": "Collect a leaf from a plant such as rhoeo, money plant, onion, hibiscus, coleus, or any grass."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.239,
            0.499,
            0.258
          ],
          "content": "Put it in a beaker filled with water."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.263,
            0.554,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": "Carefully peel a thin layer from the lower surface of the leaf."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.305,
            0.553,
            0.325
          ],
          "content": "Place the peel in a watch glass with water."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.33,
            0.554,
            0.367
          ],
          "content": "Now, take a microscope slide and carefully put a drop of water on it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.372,
            0.556,
            0.429
          ],
          "content": "Using forceps, transfer the peel of the leaf from the watch glass to the slide with the help of forceps."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.433,
            0.554,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": "Put a drop of ink on the leaf peel with the help of a dropper."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.475,
            0.554,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": "Cover the peel with a coverslip and observe it under a microscope."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.518,
            0.558,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": "What do you observe? Do you notice tiny pores on the peel, as shown in Fig. 10.6?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.563,
            0.168,
            0.895,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.598,
                0.462,
                0.848,
                0.495
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 10.6: Stomata on the lower surface of a rhoeo leaf"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.555,
            0.742,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": "These pores are called **stomata**. Stomata, present on the surface of leaves, help in the exchange of gases."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.612,
            0.486,
            0.636
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "10.3 Transport in Plants"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.66,
            0.642,
            0.682
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "10.3.1 Transport of water and minerals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.688,
            0.744,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": "All living beings need water to grow. Plants use water in the process of photosynthesis. Water, along with minerals present in the soil, is taken up by the roots of a plant. Minerals are important nutrients for the growth of plants. How do water and minerals taken up by the roots move to all parts of the plant?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.783,
            0.744,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "We can study water transport in plants by carrying out an activity. For this activity, we require two glass tumblers, some water, red ink, and twigs of two similar tender plants, preferably with white-coloured flowers (for example, white sadabahar, balsam), as shown in Fig. 10.7."
        },
        {
          "type": "aside_text",
          "bbox": [
            0.957,
            0.119,
            0.978,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "Life Processes in Plants"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.971,
            0.55,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
